  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 60af0432a3c869e48a591344a8dafd71, type: 3}
  m_Name: ClinS&E 2
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  question: 'A potential new cancer drug is being tested in a phase III clinical
    trial. As the study progresses, patient participant safety data is reviewed on
    a regular basis.


    <b>In a clinical trial, the Food and Drug Administration
    (FDA) reviews ongoing safety data and can recommend changes or stop the trial
    if necessary.</b>


    Is this <b>true</b> or <b>false</b>?'
  explanation: "While the FDA oversees and regulates clinical trials, it does not
    review day-to-day safety data. Instead, an independent Data and Safety Monitoring
    Board (DSMB)\u2014or study sponsor\u2014reviews ongoing safety information and
    can recommend changes or stop the trial if concerns arise. The DSMB helps protect
    patient participants throughout the trial by identifying risks early and ensuring
    the study remains safe and ethical."
  correctAnswer: False
  incorrectAnswer1: True
  incorrectAnswer2: 
  incorrectAnswer3: 
  questionRole: Safety & Ethics
  questionType: TrueFalse
  questionStage: Clinical
